{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'find \"$(dirname \"$(dirname \"$STAMPED_DELIVERY_MANIFEST\")\")\" -name judge-target.json -print; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" probes --help; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response --help; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" fresh-rerun --help; find /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/assessment-series/narps-neutral-r1-operational-v4/judgments/narps-neutral-r1-07-j02/attempt-01/frozen-inputs -maxdepth 3 -type f -print'","aggregated_output":"✔ The default environment has been installed.\nusage: run_probes.py [-h] [-o OUTPUT] [--response RESPONSE]\n                     [--only {ci-present,clone-weight,derived-have-records,entry-point,env-spec-tracked,inputs-untouched,large-in-annex,license-declared,modules-separated,no-abs-paths,platform-assumptions,run-env-linked,subdatasets-resolvable,tree-clean} [{ci-present,clone-weight,derived-have-records,entry-point,env-spec-tracked,inputs-untouched,large-in-annex,license-declared,modules-separated,no-abs-paths,platform-assumptions,run-env-linked,subdatasets-resolvable,tree-clean} ...]]\n                     [--skip {ci-present,clone-weight,derived-have-records,entry-point,env-spec-tracked,inputs-untouched,large-in-annex,license-declared,modules-separated,no-abs-paths,platform-assumptions,run-env-linked,subdatasets-resolvable,tree-clean} [{ci-present,clone-weight,derived-have-records,entry-point,env-spec-tracked,inputs-untouched,large-in-annex,license-declared,modules-separated,no-abs-paths,platform-assumptions,run-env-linked,subdatasets-resolvable,tree-clean} ...]]\n                     [--check-remote] [--allow GLOB [GLOB ...]]\n                     [--kind {dataset,component,study,skill}] [--overwrite]\n                     [--quiet]\n                     path\n\nRun the STAMPED probes over a checked-out research object.\n\n    run_probes.py <path> [-o probes.json] [--response response.json]\n                  [--only NAME ...] [--skip NAME ...] [--check-remote]\n                  [--kind dataset|component|study|skill]\n\nWrites the per-probe results, and optionally a response JSON (the format in\n``../stamped-conventions/references/response-format.md``) in which every item a\nprobe speaks to carries that probe's verdict and every other item is left\n``not-checked`` for the assessing session to judge.\nWithout a Git commit, ``-o`` retains a limitation receipt and exit code 2;\nno response draft is produced.\n\nThe probes never modify the object.  Nothing here replaces the judgement items:\na response straight out of this script is a draft, not an assessment.\n\npositional arguments:\n  path                  checked-out object to probe\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n  -o, --output OUTPUT   write the probe results here\n  --response RESPONSE   also write a draft response JSON here\n  --only {ci-present,clone-weight,derived-have-records,entry-point,env-spec-tracked,inputs-untouched,large-in-annex,license-declared,modules-separated,no-abs-paths,platform-assumptions,run-env-linked,subdatasets-resolvable,tree-clean} [{ci-present,clone-weight,derived-have-records,entry-point,env-spec-tracked,inputs-untouched,large-in-annex,license-declared,modules-separated,no-abs-paths,platform-assumptions,run-env-linked,subdatasets-resolvable,tree-clean} ...]\n                        run just these probes\n  --skip {ci-present,clone-weight,derived-have-records,entry-point,env-spec-tracked,inputs-untouched,large-in-annex,license-declared,modules-separated,no-abs-paths,platform-assumptions,run-env-linked,subdatasets-resolvable,tree-clean} [{ci-present,clone-weight,derived-have-records,entry-point,env-spec-tracked,inputs-untouched,large-in-annex,license-declared,modules-separated,no-abs-paths,platform-assumptions,run-env-linked,subdatasets-resolvable,tree-clean} ...]\n  --check-remote        contact submodule urls (network)\n  --allow GLOB [GLOB ...]\n                        paths no-abs-paths may skip, for files that genuinely\n                        have to carry a host path\n  --kind {dataset,component,study,skill}\n  --overwrite           replace an existing --response file instead of keeping\n                        its judged answers\n  --quiet\n✔ The default environment has been installed.\nusage: encode_response.py [-h]\n                          {items,validate,encode,decode,template,set,stamp,table} ...\n\nSTAMPED checklist responses: read the items, validate, encode, decode.\n\nThe vendored checklist in ``../checklist/`` is the only item source, and this\nscript is the only writer of a checklist-app URL.  See\n``../references/response-format.md`` for the response JSON and\n``../references/verdicts.md`` for the statuses.\n\nSubcommands\n-----------\nitems      list the pinned items (id, level, principle codes, text)\nset        answer one item in a response JSON, in place (what every run was\n           otherwise writing a merge script for)\ntable      the per-item markdown table response-format.md section 4 asks for\nstamp      fill harness, model, scope and kind; optionally hash a retained capture\nvalidate   check a response JSON against the pin and the app's constraints\nencode     response JSON -> checklist-app URL\ndecode     checklist-app URL -> response JSON (how a filled web form becomes\n           reference answers for calibration)\ntemplate   emit a response JSON skeleton with every item not-checked\n\nExit codes: 0 ok, 1 validation failed, 2 usage.\n\npositional arguments:\n  {items,validate,encode,decode,template,set,stamp,table}\n    items               list the pinned items\n    validate            check a response JSON\n    encode              response JSON -> URL\n    decode              URL -> response JSON\n    template            emit an all-not-checked response JSON\n    set                 answer one item in a response JSON, in place\n    stamp               fill harness, model, scope, kind and capture identity\n    table               print the per-item markdown table\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n✔ The default environment has been installed.\nusage: assessment.py [-h] --path PATH --entry ENTRY\n                     --outputs OUTPUTS [OUTPUTS ...] --workdir WORKDIR\n                     -o OUTPUT [--rtol RTOL] [--timeout TIMEOUT]\n                     [--max-get-gb MAX_GET_GB] [--in-container IN_CONTAINER]\n\nReplay documented outputs from a verified complete copy, never from HEAD.\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n  --path PATH\n  --entry ENTRY\n  --outputs OUTPUTS [OUTPUTS ...]\n  --workdir WORKDIR\n  -o, --output OUTPUT\n  --rtol RTOL\n  --timeout TIMEOUT\n  --max-get-gb MAX_GET_GB\n  --in-container IN_CONTAINER\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/assessment-series/narps-neutral-r1-operational-v4/judgments/narps-neutral-r1-07-j02/attempt-01/frozen-inputs/assessment-version.json\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/assessment-series/narps-neutral-r1-operational-v4/judgments/narps-neutral-r1-07-j02/attempt-01/frozen-inputs/coordinator-guidance/judging.md\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/assessment-series/narps-neutral-r1-operational-v4/judgments/narps-neutral-r1-07-j02/attempt-01/frozen-inputs/prompts/neutral-judge-operational.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'cat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/assessment-series/narps-neutral-r1-operational-v4/judgments/narps-neutral-r1-07-j02/attempt-01/frozen-inputs/coordinator-guidance/judging.md; cat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/assessment-series/narps-neutral-r1-operational-v4/judgments/narps-neutral-r1-07-j02/attempt-01/frozen-inputs/coordinator-guidance/stamped-conventions/SKILL.md 2>/dev/null; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/README.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/research-protocol.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/scientific-report.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/results/primary_v2_template_mask/summary.json\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/results/pilot_shared_support/summary.json\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/results/attempts/20261016_plot-rerun-error.md\"'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'cat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/assessment-series/narps-neutral-r1-operational-v4/judgments/narps-neutral-r1-07-j02/attempt-01/frozen-inputs/coordinator-guidance/judging.md; cat /Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/assessment-series/narps-neutral-r1-operational-v4/judgments/narps-neutral-r1-07-j02/attempt-01/frozen-inputs/coordinator-guidance/stamped-conventions/SKILL.md 2>/dev/null; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/README.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/research-protocol.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/scientific-report.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/results/primary_v2_template_mask/summary.json\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/results/pilot_shared_support/summary.json\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/results/attempts/20261016_plot-rerun-error.md\"'","aggregated_output":"# Scientific assessment context\n\nAssess the original NARPS Hypothesis 1: a positive parametric effect of potential gain in vmPFC for the equal-indifference group.\nThe original [analysis instructions](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf) require whole-brain-corrected inference.\nThe scientific task is in `input/study-task.md`.\n\nWorkers receive 54 participant gain-effect maps from team AO86 and the corresponding cohort metadata.\nPreprocessing and participant modeling are fixed; evaluate group inference conditional on those inputs.\nJudge whether the analysis handles the intended participants, image geometry, quality, gain scaling, uncertainty and anatomical interpretation appropriately.\nThe maps represent independent participants; runs have already been combined within each person.\nEvaluate exclusions, uncertainty and multiple-comparison correction against the actual method.\nDefensible approaches can reach different conclusions; matching a prior team's answer is not the criterion.\nKeep scientific validity separate from the frozen STAMPED assessment.\nCheck how image coverage and missing observations enter the analysis; whether spatial alignment, vmPFC definition, group-model assumptions and whole-brain correction support the claimed inference; and whether quality checks, uncertainty and figures make those decisions inspectable.\nDo not require a particular mask, atlas, model or numerical result.\nParticipant variance maps are absent; judge methods against the supplied data.\n# NARPS equal-indifference gain group analysis\n\nThis research object tests whether the gain-modulator effect estimate is positive in vmPFC in the 54 equal-indifference participants. Read [`scientific-report.md`](scientific-report.md) for the result and interpretation; [`research-protocol.md`](research-protocol.md) records the model and method revision.\n\n## Run\n\nFrom the workspace root, run:\n\n```bash\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/common-environment/.pixi/envs/default/bin/python analysis/analyze.py\n```\n\nThe analysis uses the supplied Python 3.13.16 environment (NumPy, SciPy, pandas, NiBabel, Matplotlib, Nilearn). It does not fetch data at run time. The two local MNI152NLin2009cAsym res-02 template files used for the analysis mask and figure are in `external/`; their retrieval URLs and SHA256 hashes are recorded in the summary JSON. The 54 fixed participant maps and cohort table, retrieval links, and hashes are recorded in `input/sources.json`. `external/nilearn-cache/icbm152_2009/` contains a symmetric 2009a template inspected during mask selection and not used in the final asymmetric 2009c analysis.\n\n## Outputs\n\n`results/primary_v2_template_mask/` contains the summary/provenance JSON, per-input QC table, unthresholded mean/t/FWE-p images, positive FWE-significant t image, top positive voxels and the FWE-significant voxel table. Values outside the analysis mask are NaN in NIfTI output. `figures/primary_v2_template_mask/` contains the whole-brain corrected map and participant-level vmPFC box plot. Run again with the command above to regenerate them.\n\n`results/pilot_shared_support/` and `figures/pilot_shared_support/` preserve the initial shared-support pilot. It was superseded after figure review showed support outside the anatomical brain; the final primary analysis applies the matching asymmetric MNI template brain mask. The pilot is retained to make the method change inspectable.\n# Group analysis protocol\n\n**Question.** In the NARPS equal-indifference group, is the mean participant gain-effect estimate positive at any location in the brain, consistent with greater response as potential gain increases?\n\n**Population and unit.** All 54 equal-indifference participants listed in `input/participants.tsv`; participant is the independent unit. No exclusions are planned. The cohort table is joined to one `sub-*_hypo1_cope.nii.gz` map by exact participant ID.\n\n**Estimand.** Voxelwise group mean of AO86's supplied participant gain-modulator contrast parameter estimates (COPEs), in their native contrast units. They are not standardized statistics, and are not interpretable as signal change per currency unit: the upstream gain regressor was mean-centered and variance-normalized before HRF convolution. Gain effects are conditional on the separate gain/loss modeling choices in `input/upstream-methods.md`.\n\n**Primary model and test.** At each voxel, a one-sample random-effects t model of the 54 participant COPEs against zero. Test only the positive direction (greater response) using sign-flip permutation of whole participant maps and a maximum t statistic across the complete analysis mask, controlling family-wise error at .05 across the brain. Use 9,999 random sign patterns and a fixed seed. The final analysis mask is the MNI152NLin2009cAsym res-02 template brain mask, nearest-neighbor resampled to the supplied image grid and intersected with finite, nonzero support in every map. This tests a population mean under exchangeability/symmetry of participant effects under the null. It is not a variance-weighted mixed-effects model because participant variance maps are unavailable.\n\n**vmPFC localization.** The primary decision is whole-brain corrected, not an ROI-only test. For a transparent descriptive summary, operationalize medial vmPFC as the midline MNI box x = -10 to +10, y = 20 to 60, z = -20 to +20 mm, intersected with the analysis mask. Report its average participant effect and uncorrected one-sample interval/test as descriptive only; it does not supply confirmatory evidence. Plot unthresholded t values with the corrected result identified separately.\n\n**Checks and uncertainty.** Verify IDs, file checksums, image shape/affine, finite support and map distributions; retain exclusions (if any) and missingness. Report the group mean, standard error, interval and one-sided whole-brain corrected p-values. As an independent numerical check, recompute a representative voxel's t and raw p with SciPy and compare to the image-derived result. Failure to reject does not establish no effect; with n=54 and absent first-level variances it may reflect limited precision or upstream model limitations.\n\n**Method revision after pilot inspection.** Initial pilot `results/pilot_shared_support/` and `figures/pilot_shared_support/` used only the intersection of map support. Visual inspection showed that some shared support was outside the anatomical brain. Before accepting the primary result, revised the mask to the matching asymmetric MNI2009c brain mask and regenerated a separately named analysis derivative. The mask and T1 reference are the TemplateFlow MNI152NLin2009cAsym res-02 files, checksummed in the result summary; the mask is available from <https://templateflow.s3.amazonaws.com/tpl-MNI152NLin2009cAsym/tpl-MNI152NLin2009cAsym_res-02_desc-brain_mask.nii.gz>.\n\n**Inputs and environment.** Fixed input bytes and their SHA256 identities are in `input/sources.json`. Do not rerun preprocessing or participant models or fetch time series. Use the supplied Python 3.13 environment with NumPy, SciPy, pandas, NiBabel and Matplotlib. Entry point: `python analysis/analyze.py` (or the prepared interpreter path in the README).\n# Potential gains and brain activity\n\n## Result\n\n**We found no support for a positive gain effect in vmPFC in the equal-indifference group under whole-brain familywise correction.** The average gain-effect estimate in the prespecified descriptive vmPFC midline box was −0.0026 COPE units (95% t interval −0.0219 to 0.0167; n = 54). Its one-sided, uncorrected group test was t(53) = −0.27, p = .606. The most positive voxel in that box was at MNI (−2, 24, −18), t = 4.19, but its whole-brain max-T corrected p = .774.\n\nThe whole-brain test did find 24 positive voxels in three small 26-connected clusters (descriptive extent) at corrected p ≤ .05. The peak was t(53) = 6.62 at MNI (−26, −102, −3.6), raw one-sided p = 9.3 × 10⁻⁹, max-T FWE p = .0006. These voxels are far posterior to the vmPFC definition and therefore do not support the stated vmPFC hypothesis. The original NARPS instructions requested binary hypothesis decisions based on whole-brain-corrected analyses ([instructions, Hypothesis 1](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf)).\n\n![Positive whole-brain max-T FWE-corrected t statistics](figures/primary_v2_template_mask/group_t_map.png)\n\n*Figure 1. Positive t statistics surviving whole-brain max-T permutation FWE p < .05, shown on the MNI152NLin2009cAsym T1 template. The corrected locations are posterior; no vmPFC voxel survives correction. A complete unthresholded t image is in `results/primary_v2_template_mask/group_t_positive.nii.gz`.*\n\n![Participant vmPFC-box effects](figures/primary_v2_template_mask/vmPFC_participant_effects.png)\n\n*Figure 2. One mean COPE per participant in the fixed vmPFC box (x = −10 to 10, y = 20 to 60, z = −20 to 20 mm). The diamond and whiskers show the mean and 95% t interval. This descriptive ROI summary is not a second confirmatory test.*\n\n## Analysis\n\nThe 54 equal-indifference participants listed in `input/participants.tsv` were the independent units. Each supplied map is AO86's unthresholded participant gain-modulator contrast parameter estimate (COPE), averaged over four runs. The map is not a t/z statistic or group effect. The upstream gain modulator was mean-centered and variance-normalized before HRF convolution, so COPE units do not represent change per currency unit. The effect is conditional on the upstream separate gain and loss models and task regressors (`input/upstream-methods.md`). The analysis does not claim that raising gain alone causes a neural response independent of other gamble attributes.\n\nWe fit a voxelwise one-sample random-effects t model against zero and tested the positive direction with 9,999 whole-participant sign flips (seed 20261016). Familywise error was controlled over the whole brain by the maximum positive t statistic at each permutation, α = .05. The test requires participant effects to be exchangeable and reasonably symmetric under the null. The analysis mask was the asymmetric MNI152NLin2009c brain mask, nearest-neighbor resampled to the supplied image grid, intersected with finite nonzero support in every participant map (186,192 of 196,466 template brain voxels; 94.8%). The template mask is from [TemplateFlow](https://github.com/templateflow/tpl-MNI152NLin2009cAsym); its exact files and SHA256 hashes are recorded in `results/primary_v2_template_mask/summary.json`.\n\nvmPFC was identified before testing as a midline anterior and ventral MNI box (|x| ≤ 10, y = 20–60, z = −20–20 mm). This is an explicit, broad operational anatomical definition rather than a functionally selected ROI. The hypothesis decision still uses whole-brain correction; the ROI mean and voxel results are descriptive, and the ROI test is uncorrected.\n\n## Quality checks and limitations\n\nAll 54 IDs matched exactly, with no duplicate participants, exclusions, nonfinite voxels, mismatched image grids, or input checksum failures. The analysis retains per-map checksums and counts in `results/primary_v2_template_mask/input_qc.csv`. An independent SciPy calculation matched the peak voxel's t statistic and raw p-value; SciPy also reproduced the full-brain maximum statistics for ten sampled sign permutations. A direct exceedance count matched the max-T corrected peak p-value. Computation and figure rendering completed in the supplied Python 3.13.16 environment.\n\nThe absent participant variance maps prevent a variance-weighted mixed-effects analysis and limit modeling of uncertainty in the upstream subject estimates. One-sample t inference treats the participant COPEs as the second-level observations; heterogeneous first-level precision may reduce efficiency or affect calibration. The shared-support mask drops voxels zero in any participant, including any zeros that might be genuine estimates, to avoid treating zero-padded or unsupported locations as observed values. This leaves 94.8% of the template brain mask.\n\nThe vmPFC interval spans zero and is compatible with small effects of either sign. **Failure to reject the vmPFC hypothesis is not evidence that vmPFC has exactly no gain response**; it means these data and this model do not establish a positive vmPFC mean at the specified whole-brain-corrected standard. The finding is also specific to the equal-indifference group, fixed AO86 first-level maps, gain-modulator scaling and vmPFC definition. No preprocessing, participant modeling or time-series analysis was repeated.\n\n## Reproducibility\n\nSee [`README.md`](README.md) for the executable command and output inventory, [`research-protocol.md`](research-protocol.md) for the recorded protocol and mask revision, and [`analysis/analyze.py`](analysis/analyze.py) for the complete analysis. Numeric outputs, thresholds, software versions, input identities and validation details are in `results/primary_v2_template_mask/summary.json`.\n{\n  \"analysis\": \"one-sample random-effects t; one-sided positive whole-brain max-T sign-flip permutation\",\n  \"n_participants\": 54,\n  \"excluded_participants\": [],\n  \"n_map_voxels_common_nonzero_support\": 186192,\n  \"participant_metadata\": {\n    \"group\": \"equalIndifference\",\n    \"age_mean\": 26.055555555555557,\n    \"age_sd\": 3.0493838948818466,\n    \"age_range\": [\n      21,\n      37\n    ],\n    \"gender_counts_as_recorded\": {\n      \"F\": 30,\n      \"M\": 24\n    }\n  },\n  \"permutations\": 9999,\n  \"seed\": 20261016,\n  \"familywise_alpha\": 0.05,\n  \"whole_brain_positive_fwe_voxels\": 24,\n  \"max_t_voxel\": {\n    \"ijk\": [\n      35,\n      15,\n      31\n    ],\n    \"mni_mm\": [\n      -26.0,\n      -102.0,\n      -3.6\n    ],\n    \"t\": 6.61717324630224,\n    \"raw_one_sided_p\": 9.311564027968546e-09,\n    \"maxT_fwe_p\": 0.0006,\n    \"fwe_p_mc_resolution\": 0.0001\n  },\n  \"vmPFC_definition\": \"MNI box |x|<=10, y=20..60, z=-20..20 mm, intersected with final analysis mask\",\n  \"vmPFC_voxels\": 3683,\n  \"vmPFC_group_mean_effect\": -0.0026102965114294724,\n  \"vmPFC_group_sd\": 0.07084479325642089,\n  \"vmPFC_group_se\": 0.009640755245066546,\n  \"vmPFC_mean_95ci\": [\n    -0.021947202736061436,\n    0.01672660971320249\n  ],\n  \"vmPFC_descriptive_t\": -0.2707564340216226,\n  \"vmPFC_descriptive_one_sided_p_uncorrected\": 0.6061849769168852,\n  \"vmPFC_peak_t\": 4.1918477635940805,\n  \"vmPFC_peak_mni_mm\": [\n    -2.0,\n    24.0,\n    -18.0\n  ],\n  \"vmPFC_peak_maxT_fwe_p\": 0.7742,\n  \"global_map_value_range\": [\n    -5.6671142578125,\n    4.776952743530273\n  ],\n  \"participant_map_mean_range\": [\n    -0.12559821942933624,\n    0.10198566892401734\n  ],\n  \"max_mean_map_sd\": 1.0005457724030198,\n  \"mask_definition\": \"MNI152NLin2009cAsym res-02 brain mask (nearest-neighbor resampled to the supplied grid), intersected with